# Git Workflow & Branch Naming Conventions

## Overview

This project follows a structured Git workflow with specific branch naming conventions to maintain organization and clarity in the development process.

## Branch Naming Conventions

### Format

```
<prefix>-<descriptive-name>
```

### Prefixes

| Prefix   | Use Case                       | Example                         |
| :------- | :----------------------------- | :------------------------------ |
| task     | General tasks and improvements | `task-update-dependencies`      |
| feature  | New features or functionality  | `feature-user-authentication`   |
| fix      | Bug fixes and corrections      | `fix-navigation-overflow`       |
| docs     | Documentation updates          | `docs-update-api-reference`     |
| refactor | Code refactoring               | `refactor-service-architecture` |
| perf     | Performance improvements       | `perf-optimize-image-loading`   |
| test     | Adding or updating tests       | `test-add-component-tests`      |
| chore    | Maintenance tasks              | `chore-update-eslint-config`    |
| ci       | CI/CD workflow changes         | `ci-add-deployment-pipeline`    |

### Naming Guidelines

#### Do's ✅

- Use lowercase letters
- Use hyphens to separate words
- Be descriptive but concise
- Use imperative mood (e.g., "add" not "adding")
- Keep it under 50 characters when possible

#### Don'ts ❌

- Don't use spaces (use hyphens instead)
- Don't use special characters except hyphens
- Don't use vague names like `fix-bug` or `update-stuff`
- Don't include issue numbers in branch name (use PR description instead)

## Examples

### Good Branch Names ✅

```bash
git checkout -b task-setup-testing-infrastructure
git checkout -b feature-dark-mode-toggle
git checkout -b fix-mobile-menu-overflow
git checkout -b docs-add-api-documentation
git checkout -b refactor-authentication-logic
git checkout -b perf-optimize-bundle-size
git checkout -b test-add-form-validation-tests
```

### Bad Branch Names ❌

```bash
git checkout -b fix-bug              # Too vague
git checkout -b Feature/New-Thing    # Wrong case/separator
git checkout -b update stuff         # Has spaces
git checkout -b fix#123              # Special characters
git checkout -b myBranch             # Not descriptive
```

## Contribution Workflow

### 1. Fork the Project

Fork the repository to your GitHub account.

### 2. Clone Your Fork

```bash
git clone https://github.com/YOUR_USERNAME/safeai4u.git
cd safeai4u
```

### 3. Create Your Branch

Choose the appropriate prefix based on your work:

```bash
# For general tasks
git checkout -b task-my-awesome-task

# For new features
git checkout -b feature-my-amazing-feature

# For bug fixes
git checkout -b fix-my-awesome-fix

# For documentation
git checkout -b docs-update-readme
```

### 4. Make Your Changes

- Write clean, maintainable code
- Follow the project's coding conventions
- Add tests if applicable
- Update documentation if needed

### 5. Commit Your Changes

Follow the [Conventional Commits](mdc:.cursor/rules/conventional-commits.mdc) guidelines:

```bash
# Stage your changes
git add .

# Commit with conventional commit format
git commit -m "feat: ✨ add dark mode toggle"
```

### 6. Keep Your Branch Updated

```bash
# Fetch latest changes from upstream
git fetch upstream

# Rebase your branch on top of dev
git rebase upstream/dev

# Or merge if you prefer
git merge upstream/dev
```

### 7. Push to Your Fork

```bash
git push origin task-my-awesome-task
```

### 8. Open a Pull Request

Create a pull request from your branch to the `dev` branch:

**PR Requirements:**

- [ ] Clear title following conventional commit format
- [ ] Summary of changes in the description
- [ ] Screenshots or GIFs if applicable (UI changes)
- [ ] Links to related issues or documentation
- [ ] Self-assign the PR
- [ ] Request review from project maintainer(s)
- [ ] Ensure all CI checks pass

## Pull Request Template

```markdown
## Description

Brief description of the changes

## Type of Change

- [ ] Bug fix (non-breaking change which fixes an issue)
- [ ] New feature (non-breaking change which adds functionality)
- [ ] Breaking change (fix or feature that would cause existing functionality to not work as expected)
- [ ] Documentation update

## Changes Made

- Change 1
- Change 2
- Change 3

## Screenshots (if applicable)

Add screenshots or GIFs here

## Related Issues

Closes #123
Refs #456

## Checklist

- [ ] My code follows the project's style guidelines
- [ ] I have performed a self-review of my code
- [ ] I have commented my code where necessary
- [ ] I have updated the documentation accordingly
- [ ] My changes generate no new warnings or errors
- [ ] I have added tests that prove my fix/feature works
- [ ] New and existing unit tests pass locally
```

## Branch Protection Rules

### Main Branch

- **Protected**: Direct pushes not allowed
- **Requires**: PR approval from maintainers
- **Requires**: All CI checks to pass
- **Target**: Production deployments

### Dev Branch

- **Protected**: Direct pushes not allowed
- **Requires**: PR approval
- **Requires**: All CI checks to pass
- **Target**: Staging/development deployments

### Feature Branches

- **Unprotected**: Direct pushes allowed
- **Base**: Should branch from `dev`
- **Merge Target**: `dev` branch
- **Cleanup**: Delete after PR is merged

## Common Scenarios

### Working on Multiple Features

```bash
# Switch back to dev first
git checkout dev
git pull origin dev

# Create new branch
git checkout -b feature-second-feature
```

### Fixing Bugs in Production

```bash
# Create hotfix from main
git checkout main
git pull origin main
git checkout -b fix-critical-security-issue

# After fixing and testing
git push origin fix-critical-security-issue
# Open PR to main (and cherry-pick to dev if needed)
```

### Updating Your Branch with Latest Changes

```bash
# If you haven't pushed yet
git checkout dev
git pull origin dev
git checkout your-branch
git rebase dev

# If you've already pushed
git checkout dev
git pull origin dev
git checkout your-branch
git merge dev
```

### Resolving Conflicts

```bash
# During rebase/merge
# 1. Fix conflicts in your editor
# 2. Stage resolved files
git add .

# 3. Continue rebase
git rebase --continue

# Or continue merge
git commit
```

## Best Practices

### Commit Frequency

- Commit often with meaningful messages
- Each commit should represent a logical unit of work
- Don't commit broken code to shared branches

### Branch Lifespan

- Keep branches short-lived (days, not weeks)
- Merge or close stale branches
- Delete merged branches

### Code Review

- Request reviews promptly
- Respond to feedback constructively
- Make requested changes in the same branch

### Testing

- Test your changes locally before pushing
- Ensure all tests pass
- Add new tests for new features

## Git Configuration

### Recommended Git Aliases

```bash
# Add to ~/.gitconfig
[alias]
    co = checkout
    br = branch
    ci = commit
    st = status
    unstage = reset HEAD --
    last = log -1 HEAD
    visual = log --oneline --graph --decorate --all
```

### Useful Commands

```bash
# View branch structure
git log --oneline --graph --decorate --all

# List all branches
git branch -a

# Delete local branch
git branch -d branch-name

# Delete remote branch
git push origin --delete branch-name

# Rename current branch
git branch -m new-branch-name
```

